Q: Why can my non-Sendable closure cross the isolation boundary A: <p>Just picking up on what I was getting at with my comments:</p>
<p>The way to know a limit, as Wittgenstein says somewhere, is to walk both sides of the limit. What would your code have to look like in order for the compiler to complain? The simplest change is to remove the <code>@MainActor</code> designation on the closure type:</p>
<pre><code>actor MyActor {
    var checker: () -&gt; Void = {}

    init(checker: @escaping () -&gt; Void) {
        self.checker = checker
    }

    nonisolated func noniso() {
        Task {
            let checker = await self.checker // error
            checker()
        }
    }
}
</code></pre>
<p>Now the compiler stops us inside <code>noniso</code>:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Non-Sendable type <code>() -&gt; Void</code> of property <code>checker</code> cannot exit actor-isolated context</p>
</blockquote>
<p>So now we know that the <code>@MainActor</code> designation <em>is at least tantamount to a <code>@Sendable</code> designation</em>, since with it, the compiler does <em>not</em> complain that <code>checker</code> is Non-Sendable.</p>
<p>Of course, actually saying <code>@Sendable</code> would do as well:</p>
<pre><code>actor MyActor {
    var checker: @Sendable () -&gt; Void = {}

    init(checker: @escaping @Sendable () -&gt; Void) {
        self.checker = checker
    }

    nonisolated func noniso() {
        Task {
            let checker = await self.checker // fine
            checker()
        }
    }
}
</code></pre>
<p>Having said all that, it may also help to be clear on what a <code>@Sendable</code> closure <em>means</em>. Designating the closure <code>@Sendable</code> doesn't take the guard rails down; it puts them up. The compiler will now help us to ensure that the closure really <em>is</em> Sendable. But this is not obvious, because your example doesn't include enough code. &quot;Enough code&quot; would consist of actually <em>calling</em> the initializer and providing an <em>actual</em> closure. For example:</p>
<pre><code>class ViewController: UIViewController {
    var testing = &quot;testing&quot;
    override func viewDidLoad() {
        super.viewDidLoad()
        let myActor = MyActor { print(self.testing) } // warning
        myActor.noniso()
    }
}
</code></pre>
<p>That elicits a warning (I do not understand why it's not an outright error):</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Main actor-isolated property <code>testing</code> can not be referenced from a Sendable closure</p>
</blockquote>
<p>The point is that it is its <em>captures</em> that are important to the sendability of a closure. If we grab a <em>copy</em> of <code>testing</code> by means of the closure's capture list, that's perfectly okay:</p>
<pre><code>        let myActor = MyActor { [mytesting = testing] in print(mytesting) }
</code></pre>
<p>Similarly, if <code>testing</code> had been a <code>let</code> constant instead of a <code>var</code> variable, that would have been fine too, because a String is Sendable.</p>
<p>And now let's go back to your original formulation, with <code>@MainActor</code>. In that case, our capture is perfectly fine:</p>
<pre><code>class ViewController: UIViewController {
    var testing = &quot;testing&quot;
    override func viewDidLoad() {
        super.viewDidLoad()
        let myActor = MyActor { print(self.testing) } // fine
        myActor.noniso()
    }
}

actor MyActor {
    var checker: @MainActor () -&gt; Void = {}

    init(checker: @escaping @MainActor () -&gt; Void) {
        self.checker = checker
    }

    nonisolated func noniso() {
        Task {
            let checker = await self.checker
            await checker()
        }
    }
}
</code></pre>
<p>In that case we're capturing a variable property from within a closure that is known to run on the main actor, the same actor to which that variable property is isolated.</p>
<p>In this sense, then, your choice of <code>@MainActor</code> might be seen as painting with an even broader brush than <code>@Sendable</code> would have been.</p>

Q: Saving Data from Laravel Form in Bootstrap Modal A: <p>They are quiet a few problem i noticed on your code some of which have already been answered by the past contributors, but here is mine</p>
<p>1. The problem is a structural issue with your modal HTML, the <code>&lt;/form&gt;</code> tag is misplaced, which causes the submit button to fall <strong>outside</strong> the form entirely.</p>
<pre><code>&lt;div class=&quot;modal-body&quot;&gt;
    {{ Form::open(...) }}   &lt;!-- form opens here --&gt;
    ...fields...
    &lt;/form&gt;                 &lt;!-- form closes here (TOO EARLY) --&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;modal-footer&quot;&gt;
    {{ Form::submit(...) }} &lt;!-- submit button is OUTSIDE the form! --&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
{{ Form::close() }} 
</code></pre>
<p>Because the submit button is outside the <code>&lt;form&gt;</code> element, clicking it does nothing — no POST is ever sent.</p>
<p><strong>Suggestion:</strong> Move <code>Form::open()</code> and <code>Form::close()</code> so they wrap the <strong>entire modal content</strong>, including the footer:</p>
<pre><code>&lt;div id=&quot;addPenggunaModal&quot; class=&quot;modal hide fade&quot; tabindex=&quot;-1&quot; role=&quot;dialog&quot;&gt;

  {{ Form::open(array('url'=&gt;'users/addpengguna','class'=&gt;'form-horizontal','method'=&gt;'POST')) }}

  &lt;div class=&quot;modal-header&quot;&gt;
      &lt;button type=&quot;button&quot; class=&quot;close&quot; data-dismiss=&quot;modal&quot;&gt;×&lt;/button&gt;
      &lt;h3&gt;Tambah Pengguna Baru&lt;/h3&gt;
  &lt;/div&gt;

  &lt;div class=&quot;modal-body&quot;&gt;
      &lt;ul&gt;
          @foreach($errors-&gt;all() as $error)
              &lt;li&gt;{{ $error }}&lt;/li&gt;
          @endforeach
      &lt;/ul&gt;
      {{-- your fields here --}}
  &lt;/div&gt;

  &lt;div class=&quot;modal-footer&quot;&gt;
      {{ Form::submit('Simpan', array('class'=&gt;'btn btn-primary')) }}
      &lt;button class=&quot;btn&quot; data-dismiss=&quot;modal&quot;&gt;Batal&lt;/button&gt;
  &lt;/div&gt;

  {{ Form::close() }}

&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div id=&quot;addPenggunaModal&quot; class=&quot;modal hide fade&quot; tabindex=&quot;-1&quot; role=&quot;dialog&quot;&gt;

  {{ Form::open(array('url'=&gt;'users/addpengguna','class'=&gt;'form-horizontal','method'=&gt;'POST')) }}

  &lt;div class=&quot;modal-header&quot;&gt;
      &lt;button type=&quot;button&quot; class=&quot;close&quot; data-dismiss=&quot;modal&quot;&gt;×&lt;/button&gt;
      &lt;h3&gt;Tambah Pengguna Baru&lt;/h3&gt;
  &lt;/div&gt;

  &lt;div class=&quot;modal-body&quot;&gt;
      &lt;ul&gt;
          @foreach($errors-&gt;all() as $error)
              &lt;li&gt;{{ $error }}&lt;/li&gt;
          @endforeach
      &lt;/ul&gt;
      {{-- your fields here --}}
  &lt;/div&gt;

  &lt;div class=&quot;modal-footer&quot;&gt;
      {{ Form::submit('Simpan', array('class'=&gt;'btn btn-primary')) }}
      &lt;button class=&quot;btn&quot; data-dismiss=&quot;modal&quot;&gt;Batal&lt;/button&gt;
  &lt;/div&gt;

  {{ Form::close() }}

&lt;/div&gt;
</code></pre>
<p><strong>To answer your three questions:</strong></p>
<p><strong>1. Tracing POST requests</strong>: No  you don't need any browser extension. Just Open your browser's built-in DevTools (<code>F12</code>) → <strong>Network tab</strong> → submit the form → click the POST request → check the <strong>Payload</strong> tab to see what data was actually sent (or if anything was sent at all).</p>
<p><strong>2. Laravel error logs</strong>: Since inception laravel logs are always stored in <code>app/storage/logs/laravel.log</code>. Also make sure <code>'debug' =&gt; true</code> is set in <code>app/config/app.php</code> during development so errors display in the browser.</p>
<p><strong>3. Also fix your controller's failure redirect:</strong></p>
<p>php</p>
<pre><code>} else {
    return Redirect::back()-&gt;withErrors($validator)-&gt;withInput();
}
</code></pre>
<p>Using <code>Redirect::to('index')</code> on failure loses the validation errors and the user's input. <code>Redirect::back()</code> returns them to the same page with errors intact, so your <code>@foreach($errors-&gt;all())</code> will actually display something.</p>
<p><strong>Regarding your AJAX + missing token error:</strong> <code>Form::open()</code> automatically injects a hidden <code>_token</code> field for CSRF protection. If you use AJAX instead, you need to pass it manually:</p>
<pre><code>$.ajax({
    url: '/users/addpengguna',
    type: 'POST',
    data: {
        _token: $('input[name=&quot;_token&quot;]').val(),
        firstname: $('#firstname').val(),
        // ... other fields
    }
});
</code></pre>
<p>what this is doing is simply grabbing the value from an hidden input named _token in your HTML DOM <code>input[name=&quot;_token&quot;]</code> and passing it onto your request /users/addpengguna laravel is wired in such a way that CSRF would work if it detects a _token field for a POST web request, otherwise it would fail, keep in mind tho that csrf_token does have an expiry time, after which your Ajax request might become stall (would not be able to send through), and you might need to refresh the page or handle that edge cases for it to work.</p>
<p>Good luck.</p>
